   "adv": false,
   "html": "<h2>What it is</h2>\n<p>A bank deposit is a promise by your bank. If the bank can't keep it, a <strong>chain of backstops</strong> is supposed to step in, roughly in this order:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Order</th>\n<th>Link</th>\n<th>What it offers</th>\n<th>Page</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><strong>The bank itself</strong></td>\n<td>Its own capital and liquid assets</td>\n<td><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/bank-capital-and-liquidity\" data-page=\"bank-capital-and-liquidity\">Bank Capital and Liquidity</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><strong>The central bank</strong></td>\n<td>Emergency lending while the bank is only short of cash</td>\n<td><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/lender-of-last-resort\" data-page=\"lender-of-last-resort\">Lender of Last Resort</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td><strong>The bank's investors</strong></td>\n<td>Losses imposed on <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/shares\" data-page=\"shares\">shareholders</a>, <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/bonds\" data-page=\"bonds\">bondholders</a> and large depositors</td>\n<td><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/bail-in\" data-page=\"bail-in\">Bail-in and the Creditor Ladder</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td><strong>Deposit insurance</strong></td>\n<td>A fund that repays deposits up to a limit</td>\n<td><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/deposit-insurance\" data-page=\"deposit-insurance\">Deposit Insurance</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td><strong>The government</strong></td>\n<td>Public money to rescue a bank, or to back the insurance fund</td>\n<td><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/bank-sovereign-loop\" data-page=\"bank-sovereign-loop\">The Bank–Government Loop</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2>Why it matters</h2>\n<p>Whether you lose money in a bank failure depends on <strong>which link breaks</strong>, and whether the next link is strong enough to catch it. A small bank failing in a large, solvent country is caught early in the chain. A banking system bigger than its own government can bring the whole chain down.</p>\n<h2>How it works</h2>\n<p>Each link is only as good as its size relative to the problem:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A bank with thick capital absorbs losses itself.</li>\n<li>A central bank can lend unlimited amounts of its own currency to banks that are solvent but short of cash; it shouldn't lend to banks whose losses exceed their capital.</li>\n<li>Where the law says so, losses then go to the bank's investors and large depositors before taxpayers.</li>\n<li>Deposit insurance repays small depositors; it handles one or two failures, not a whole system.</li>\n<li>The government can borrow to rescue banks — if it can borrow at all.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Follow the chain step by step in the flow <em>When a bank fails</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Real cases:</strong>\n| Case | What happened | Which link held |\n|---|---|---|\n| United States, 2023 | Silicon Valley Bank failed after losses on bonds and a fast run | The government and insurance fund protected <strong>all</strong> deposits, including those above the limit |\n| Iceland, 2008 | Three banks with assets of about 14 times the country's annual output collapsed | Domestic deposits were guaranteed; depositors in foreign branches lost access, and a long dispute followed |\n| Cyprus, 2013 | Two large banks failed while the government couldn't borrow | Insured deposits were protected; large deposits took heavy losses |\n| Switzerland, 2023 | Credit Suisse was taken over by UBS | Depositors were protected; about 16 billion Swiss francs of the bank's riskiest bonds were written to zero, a decision a Swiss court later ruled unlawful (under appeal as of early 2026) |\n| Lebanon, from 2019 | Banks restricted withdrawals as the state's finances collapsed | No link held: deposits were frozen for years |</p>\n<h2>What goes wrong</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The chain is only as strong as its last link.</strong> Insurance funds hold a small fraction of the deposits they cover, so in a systemic crisis the government behind them is what really matters.</li>\n<li><strong>The links are tied together.</strong> Banks often hold large amounts of their own government's debt, so a weak government weakens its banks and the other way round.</li>\n<li><strong>Rescues aren't guaranteed.</strong> Whether uninsured depositors are protected is decided at the time, case by case.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Key rule:</strong> a deposit is only as safe as the strongest link still standing behind it. Ask how big each link is compared with what it might have to cover.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2>Connects to</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/bank-deposits\" data-page=\"bank-deposits\">Bank Deposits</a> — the promise the chain protects.</li>\n<li><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/hierarchy-of-money\" data-page=\"hierarchy-of-money\">The Hierarchy of Money</a> — each backstop is a lower, stronger layer of money standing behind the one above.</li>\n<li><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/cartography-of-wealth\" data-page=\"cartography-of-wealth\">The Cartography of Wealth</a> — deposits are Tier 3: protected by the state, and therefore exposed to it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Check yourself</h2>\n<div class=\"qa\"><p class=\"q\">Why could the United States protect every depositor at a failed bank in 2023, when Iceland couldn't do the same for foreign depositors in 2008?</p><details><summary>Show answer</summary><p>Because of relative size. The failed US banks were small compared with the US government's ability to borrow; Iceland's banks were many times larger than its whole economy.</p></details></div>\n<div class=\"qa\"><p class=\"q\">Deposit insurance covers your deposit. Why does the government's own health still matter?</p><details><summary>Show answer</summary><p>Because insurance funds hold only a small fraction of the deposits they cover. In a systemic crisis the fund needs the government behind it, and if the government can't borrow, the promise weakens.</p></details></div>",
