   "text": "What it is A bail out rescues a bank with public money. A bail in rescues it by imposing the losses on the bank's own investors and creditors: their claims are written down or turned into shares until the bank has enough capital again. Why it exists After the 2008 crisis, many governments decided taxpayers shouldn't keep rescuing banks. Resolution laws — in the European Union, the United Kingdom, Canada and elsewhere — now set out how a failing bank's own creditors absorb the losses first. How it works Losses move up a creditor ladder . Each rung is wiped out before the next is touched: Order Who takes the loss 1 Shareholders 2 Holders of the bank's riskiest bonds, issued to be written off in a crisis 3 Other junior bondholders 4 Senior bondholders and other unsecured creditors 5 Large deposits above the insurance limit (in many systems, those of individuals and small firms rank ahead of those of big companies) — Insured deposits are protected The exact order varies by country and by the bank's own legal terms. Real cases: Cyprus, 2013. At Bank of Cyprus, 47.5% of large deposits above the €100,000 insured limit were turned into shares; at the second large bank, uninsured depositors bore most of the losses. Insured deposits were protected. Credit Suisse, 2023. In a government arranged takeover by UBS, about 16 billion Swiss francs of the bank's riskiest bonds (AT1 bonds) were written to zero, while shareholders still received UBS shares — a controversial break with the usual order. In October 2025 Switzerland's Federal Administrative Court ruled that the write off lacked a sufficient legal basis. The regulator and UBS appealed to the Federal Supreme Court, and as of early 2026 the bonds stayed written down while the appeal ran. A rule that looks clear on paper can still be tested in court for years. What goes wrong Large depositors are creditors , not owners of cash in a vault. Above the insurance limit, a deposit can be bailed in. The order can be changed by law or by the bond's own terms, as Credit Suisse bondholders found. Contagion. Fear of bail in can trigger the very run it is meant to prevent. Key rule: above the insurance limit, a deposit is an unsecured loan to the bank, and in a failure it can be turned into shares or written down. Check yourself Q: In a bail in, who should take losses before large depositors? A: Shareholders first, then holders of the bank's riskiest bonds, then other bondholders and unsecured creditors. Large deposits come near the end, and insured deposits are protected.",
