   "adv": false,
   "html": "<h2>What it is</h2>\n<p>A <strong>bond</strong> is a loan broken into tradable pieces. The borrower (the <strong>issuer</strong>) promises to pay interest at set times and repay the loan on a set date.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Face value</strong></td>\n<td>The amount repaid at the end (often 100 or 1,000 per bond)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Coupon</strong></td>\n<td>The interest rate on the face value, paid regularly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Maturity</strong></td>\n<td>The date the face value is repaid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Issuer</strong></td>\n<td>Who owes the money: a government, a company, a city</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2>Why it exists</h2>\n<p>Bonds let borrowers raise large sums for long periods from many lenders at once, and let those lenders sell to someone else instead of waiting for repayment.</p>\n<h2>How it works</h2>\n<p><strong>Main kinds of issuer:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Governments</strong> borrowing in their own currency: usually the lowest-risk bonds in that currency.</li>\n<li><strong>Local governments and agencies:</strong> backed by taxes or by specific projects.</li>\n<li><strong>Companies:</strong> higher yields to pay for the risk that the company fails. See <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/credit-risk\" data-page=\"credit-risk\">Credit Risk and Ratings</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Main kinds of bond:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fixed-rate:</strong> the same coupon every period.</li>\n<li><strong>Floating-rate:</strong> the coupon resets with a benchmark interest rate.</li>\n<li><strong>Zero-coupon:</strong> no coupons; bought at a discount and repaid at face value, like a long <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/t-bills\" data-page=\"t-bills\">T-bill</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Inflation-linked:</strong> payments rise with prices. See <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/inflation-linked-bonds\" data-page=\"inflation-linked-bonds\">Inflation-Linked Bonds</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Where bondholders stand.</strong> If a company fails, bondholders are paid before shareholders. Secured bonds, backed by specific assets, are paid before unsecured ones. See <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/claims-and-liabilities\" data-page=\"claims-and-liabilities\">Claims and Liabilities</a>.</p>\n<h2>What goes wrong</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Default:</strong> the issuer misses payments. Bondholders may get back only part of what they're owed.</li>\n<li><strong>Rate rises</strong> cut the market price. See <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/bond-pricing\" data-page=\"bond-pricing\">Bond Prices and Yields</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Inflation</strong> erodes fixed payments.</li>\n<li><strong>Early repayment:</strong> some bonds can be repaid early by the issuer, usually when rates have fallen and you'd least want your money back.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Key rule:</strong> a bond is a promise. Judge the promiser first, then the price.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2>How it has failed</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Repudiation, 1918.</strong> After the revolution, Russia's new government refused to honour the Tsarist state's debts. Foreign bondholders lost almost everything.</li>\n<li><strong>Argentina, 2001.</strong> Argentina defaulted on its government debt, then the largest sovereign default on record, and has restructured its debt several times since.</li>\n<li><strong>Greece, 2012.</strong> Private holders of Greek government bonds took a 53.5% cut to the face value of their bonds, reducing Greece's debt by about €107 billion.</li>\n<li><strong>Inflation.</strong> Bonds can fail without a default. In the high inflation of the 1970s, holders of long-dated bonds were repaid in full in money that bought far less. See <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/inflation\" data-page=\"inflation\">Inflation</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Check yourself</h2>\n<div class=\"qa\"><p class=\"q\">A company fails. Who is paid first: its bondholders or its shareholders?</p><details><summary>Show answer</summary><p>Its bondholders. Shareholders own what's left only after every creditor, including bondholders, has been paid.</p></details></div>",
