   "text": "What it is A bond is a loan broken into tradable pieces. The borrower (the issuer ) promises to pay interest at set times and repay the loan on a set date. Term Meaning Face value The amount repaid at the end (often 100 or 1,000 per bond) Coupon The interest rate on the face value, paid regularly Maturity The date the face value is repaid Issuer Who owes the money: a government, a company, a city Why it exists Bonds let borrowers raise large sums for long periods from many lenders at once, and let those lenders sell to someone else instead of waiting for repayment. How it works Main kinds of issuer: Governments borrowing in their own currency: usually the lowest risk bonds in that currency. Local governments and agencies: backed by taxes or by specific projects. Companies: higher yields to pay for the risk that the company fails. See credit risk. Main kinds of bond: Fixed rate: the same coupon every period. Floating rate: the coupon resets with a benchmark interest rate. Zero coupon: no coupons; bought at a discount and repaid at face value, like a long T bill. Inflation linked: payments rise with prices. See inflation linked bonds. Where bondholders stand. If a company fails, bondholders are paid before shareholders. Secured bonds, backed by specific assets, are paid before unsecured ones. See claims and liabilities. What goes wrong Default: the issuer misses payments. Bondholders may get back only part of what they're owed. Rate rises cut the market price. See bond pricing. Inflation erodes fixed payments. Early repayment: some bonds can be repaid early by the issuer, usually when rates have fallen and you'd least want your money back. Key rule: a bond is a promise. Judge the promiser first, then the price. How it has failed Repudiation, 1918. After the revolution, Russia's new government refused to honour the Tsarist state's debts. Foreign bondholders lost almost everything. Argentina, 2001. Argentina defaulted on its government debt, then the largest sovereign default on record, and has restructured its debt several times since. Greece, 2012. Private holders of Greek government bonds took a 53.5% cut to the face value of their bonds, reducing Greece's debt by about €107 billion. Inflation. Bonds can fail without a default. In the high inflation of the 1970s, holders of long dated bonds were repaid in full in money that bought far less. See inflation. Check yourself Q: A company fails. Who is paid first: its bondholders or its shareholders? A: Its bondholders. Shareholders own what's left only after every creditor, including bondholders, has been paid.",
