   "html": "<h2>What it is</h2>\n<p>The money a central bank issues. It comes in two forms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Banknotes</strong> — the physical cash the public can hold.</li>\n<li><strong>Reserves</strong> — balances that commercial banks keep in accounts at the central bank. Access is limited, mainly to banks.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Both are the <strong>central bank's liability</strong>. Together they are often called the <em>monetary base</em>.</p>\n<h2>Why it exists</h2>\n<p>Banks need a shared, final way to settle with each other. When your bank pays another bank, the final settlement is a transfer of reserves on the central bank's books. Everything above it — <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/bank-deposits\" data-page=\"bank-deposits\">Bank Deposits</a>, <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/eurodollars\" data-page=\"eurodollars\" data-adv=\"1\">Eurodollars</a>, <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/stablecoins\" data-page=\"stablecoins\">Stablecoins</a> — is ultimately a promise to pay in central bank money.</p>\n<h2>How it works</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Creation.</strong> The central bank creates reserves when it buys assets (usually government <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/bonds\" data-page=\"bonds\">Bonds</a>) or lends to banks. It removes them when it sells assets or loans are repaid.</li>\n<li><strong>Policy rates.</strong> The central bank sets the interest rate it pays on reserves and steers the rate banks charge each other for overnight loans. That rate ripples out into other interest rates. See <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/monetary-policy\" data-page=\"monetary-policy\">Monetary Policy</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Balance sheet size.</strong> Large asset purchases (\"quantitative easing\") expand reserves; letting assets run off (\"quantitative tightening\") shrinks them. This feeds directly into <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/liquidity\" data-page=\"liquidity\">Liquidity</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What goes wrong</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inflation</strong> if money is created faster than the economy can absorb it. See <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/inflation\" data-page=\"inflation\">Inflation</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Loss of trust</strong> in the central bank or government, which pushes people towards assets lower in the <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/hierarchy-of-money\" data-page=\"hierarchy-of-money\">hierarchy of money</a>, such as <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/gold\" data-page=\"gold\">Gold</a>, or towards foreign currencies.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Key rule:</strong> central bank money is the base layer for almost everything you will hold, which is why central bank decisions move every market at once.</p>\n</blockquote>",
