   "branch": "foundation",
   "hits": [],
   "adv": false,
   "html": "<h2>What it is</h2>\n<p>A <strong>claim</strong> is a right to receive something from someone. The other side of every claim is a <strong>liability</strong>: the duty to pay. Your bank deposit is your claim and the bank's liability. A bond is your claim and the borrower's liability.</p>\n<p>Some assets are <strong>nobody's liability</strong>. Gold, land, bearer crypto and your own skills don't depend on anyone keeping a promise.</p>\n<h2>Why it matters</h2>\n<p>\"Is it backed?\" is a weak test. Almost no modern money can be redeemed for anything. The stronger test is two questions:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Whose liability is it?</strong> That tells you who has to stay solvent and honest for it to keep its value.</li>\n<li><strong>What is it a claim on?</strong> That tells you what you actually get if it pays.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>How it sorts the assets</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Asset</th>\n<th>Whose liability?</th>\n<th>Claim on</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/gold\" data-page=\"gold\">Gold</a></td>\n<td>Nobody's</td>\n<td>— (it is the asset)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/central-bank-money\" data-page=\"central-bank-money\">Central Bank Money</a></td>\n<td>The central bank's</td>\n<td>Nothing redeemable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/bank-deposits\" data-page=\"bank-deposits\">Bank Deposits</a></td>\n<td>Your bank's</td>\n<td>Central bank money</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/stablecoins\" data-page=\"stablecoins\">Stablecoins</a></td>\n<td>The issuing company's</td>\n<td>Dollars held in reserve</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/bonds\" data-page=\"bonds\">Bonds</a></td>\n<td>A government or company</td>\n<td>Future money</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/shares\" data-page=\"shares\">Shares</a></td>\n<td>The company (as the residual owner)</td>\n<td>Future profits, after everyone else</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/real-estate\" data-page=\"real-estate\">Real Estate</a></td>\n<td>Nobody's</td>\n<td>Use and rent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/bitcoin\" data-page=\"bitcoin\">Bitcoin</a></td>\n<td>Nobody's</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/memecoins\" data-page=\"memecoins\">Memecoins</a></td>\n<td>Nobody's</td>\n<td>— (attention only)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Which side of a claim you hold also decides how you're paid: a creditor earns interest, an owner earns whatever profit is left. See <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/four-ways-to-earn\" data-page=\"four-ways-to-earn\">Four Ways to Earn a Return</a>.</p>\n<h2>The link to visibility</h2>\n<p>Claims have to be recorded, because both sides need proof of who owes whom. That is why claims are visible to the state. Things that are nobody's liability <em>can</em> be held privately. This is the same divide the <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/cartography-of-wealth\" data-page=\"cartography-of-wealth\">Cartography of Wealth</a> draws between legible and bearer assets.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Key rule:</strong> a claim is only as good as the party who owes it. Being nobody's liability removes that risk, but replaces it with others: theft, loss and no one to call.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2>Check yourself</h2>\n<div class=\"qa\"><p class=\"q\">You hold a stablecoin, a gold bar and a government bond. Which of them is nobody's liability?</p><details><summary>Show answer</summary><p>Only the gold bar. The stablecoin is a claim on the company that issued it, and the bond is a claim on the government. The gold bar depends on no one keeping a promise, but it can be stolen or lost.</p></details></div>\n<div class=\"qa\"><p class=\"q\">Why is \"is it backed?\" a weaker test than \"whose liability is it?\"</p><details><summary>Show answer</summary><p>Because almost no modern money can be redeemed for anything, so nearly everything fails the \"backed\" test. Asking whose liability it is shows who must stay solvent and honest for it to hold value.</p></details></div>",
