   "adv": false,
   "html": "<h2>What it is</h2>\n<p><strong>Compounding</strong> means each period's return is added to the balance, so the next period's return is earned on a bigger amount. Growth builds on growth.</p>\n<h2>Why it matters</h2>\n<p>Compounding is why the length of time money stays invested often matters more than the exact moment it goes in, and why small differences in rates — including fees — become large over decades.</p>\n<h2>How it works</h2>\n<p><strong>Simple versus compound (fictional figures):</strong> 1,000 at 5% a year for 10 years.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Method</th>\n<th>Interest is earned on</th>\n<th>Value after 10 years</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Simple</td>\n<td>The original 1,000 only</td>\n<td>1,500.00</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Compound</td>\n<td>The growing balance</td>\n<td>1,628.89</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Small differences grow large.</strong> 10,000 for 30 years:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>at 7% a year it grows to about <strong>76,122.55</strong>;</li>\n<li>at 6% a year (the same return less a 1% annual fee) it grows to about <strong>57,434.91</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The fee looks like 1%, but it removes about <strong>a quarter</strong> of the final value.</p>\n<figure class=\"fig\"><a class=\"fig-link\" href=\"assets/figures/compounding-fee-drag.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"assets/figures/compounding-fee-drag.svg\" alt=\"A line chart of $10,000 growing at 7% a year for 30 years. With no fee it reaches $76,123; with a 1% yearly fee it reaches $57,435. The $18,688 gap is about a quarter of the no-fee result.\" width=\"560\" height=\"680\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><figcaption>The same 10,000 for 30 years, at 7% a year and at 6% (7% less a 1% fee). Fictional figures.<span class=\"fig-hint\"> Tap to enlarge.</span></figcaption></figure>\n<p><strong>The rule of 72.</strong> Divide 72 by the percentage rate to estimate how many years it takes money to double. At 6%, 72 ÷ 6 = 12 years; the exact answer is about 11.9. It's a quick check, and it is most accurate for rates between roughly 4% and 12%.</p>\n<p><strong>Compounding frequency.</strong> The more often returns are added, the higher the effective yearly rate. A 12% rate added monthly works out to about 12.68% a year.</p>\n<h2>What goes wrong</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Compounding works on costs too:</strong> fees, inflation and interest on debt all compound against you.</li>\n<li><strong>Losses interrupt it.</strong> A large fall shrinks the base that future returns grow from. See <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/risk-and-return\" data-page=\"risk-and-return\">Risk and Return</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Taking money out early</strong> stops the later, largest years of growth from happening.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Key rule:</strong> in compounding, the last years do most of the work — so time in the market, low costs and avoiding large losses matter more than clever timing.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2>Connects to</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/time-value-of-money\" data-page=\"time-value-of-money\">Time Value of Money</a> — the same formula, run forwards.</li>\n<li><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/inflation\" data-page=\"inflation\">Inflation</a> — the compounding that runs against cash.</li>\n<li><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/returns\" data-page=\"returns\">Returns</a> — how annualised (compound) returns are measured.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Check yourself</h2>\n<div class=\"qa\"><p class=\"q\">Using the rule of 72, roughly how long does money take to double at 9% a year?</p><details><summary>Show answer</summary><p>About 8 years (72 ÷ 9). The exact figure is a little over 8.</p></details></div>\n<div class=\"qa\"><p class=\"q\">Why does a 1% annual fee cost far more than 1% of the final value over 30 years?</p><details><summary>Show answer</summary><p>Because each year's fee also removes the future returns that money would have earned. Over 30 years at these rates it removes about a quarter of the final value.</p></details></div>",
