   "html": "<h2>What fails</h2>\n<p><strong>Ownership itself.</strong> A state can take an asset by law: requiring citizens to hand over gold, nationalising a company or an industry, or freezing another country's or person's assets. Unlike theft, it's done in the open, under rules the owner can't override.</p>\n<h2>The backstop</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Courts and constitutions</strong>, where property rights are protected and judges are independent.</li>\n<li><strong>Compensation</strong>, sometimes paid, often less than the owner thinks the asset was worth.</li>\n<li><strong>International agreements</strong> that protect foreign investors, and arbitration to enforce them.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Where the backstop runs out</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The state writes the rules.</strong> Courts can only enforce the law as it stands, and laws can change.</li>\n<li><strong>Compensation is negotiated,</strong> and may take years.</li>\n<li><strong>Assets held abroad</strong> are under the host country's law. Holding money elsewhere protects against your own government, but exposes you to someone else's.</li>\n<li><strong>Visibility matters.</strong> The more legible an asset, the easier it is to reach. That's the vertical axis of the <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/cartography-of-wealth\" data-page=\"cartography-of-wealth\">Cartography of Wealth</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Cases</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>US gold, 1933.</strong> An executive order required citizens to hand in most gold coins, bullion and certificates at a fixed price. The dollar was then devalued against gold. See <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/gold\" data-page=\"gold\">Gold</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Mexico, 18 March 1938.</strong> Mexico expropriated foreign oil companies. Compensation was settled later: about $29 million to American firms in 1942, and $130 million to British interests in 1947.</li>\n<li><strong>Russia's reserves, 2022.</strong> After the invasion of Ukraine, Western countries froze about $300 billion of the Russian central bank's reserves, roughly half of its foreign currency and gold. Even a central bank's reserves held abroad are subject to the host countries' law.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What it kills</h2>\n<p>Assets the state can see and reach: <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/real-estate\" data-page=\"real-estate\">Real Estate</a>, <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/bank-deposits\" data-page=\"bank-deposits\">Bank Deposits</a>, <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/shares\" data-page=\"shares\">Shares</a> in strategic companies, whole <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/private-business\" data-page=\"private-business\">businesses</a> in an industry the state nationalises, and <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/gold\" data-page=\"gold\">Gold</a> where its ownership is recorded or known. <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/human-capital\" data-page=\"human-capital\">Human Capital</a> and <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/social-capital\" data-page=\"social-capital\">Social Capital</a> are the hardest to confiscate, which is why the Cartography treats them as the root store.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Key rule:</strong> every asset is held under someone's law. Know whose, and whether that law protects owners like you.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2>Check yourself</h2>\n<div class=\"qa\"><p class=\"q\">Why can holding assets abroad both reduce and add confiscation risk?</p><details><summary>Show answer</summary><p>It moves them out of reach of your own government, but puts them under the law of the country that holds them, as Russia's frozen reserves showed in 2022.</p></details></div>\n<div class=\"qa\"><p class=\"q\">How is confiscation different from theft?</p><details><summary>Show answer</summary><p>It's done openly by a state under its own laws, so courts may be unable to reverse it, and any compensation is set by the same state.</p></details></div>",
