   "text": "What fails Ownership itself. A state can take an asset by law: requiring citizens to hand over gold, nationalising a company or an industry, or freezing another country's or person's assets. Unlike theft, it's done in the open, under rules the owner can't override. The backstop Courts and constitutions , where property rights are protected and judges are independent. Compensation , sometimes paid, often less than the owner thinks the asset was worth. International agreements that protect foreign investors, and arbitration to enforce them. Where the backstop runs out The state writes the rules. Courts can only enforce the law as it stands, and laws can change. Compensation is negotiated, and may take years. Assets held abroad are under the host country's law. Holding money elsewhere protects against your own government, but exposes you to someone else's. Visibility matters. The more legible an asset, the easier it is to reach. That's the vertical axis of the Cartography of Wealth. Cases US gold, 1933. An executive order required citizens to hand in most gold coins, bullion and certificates at a fixed price. The dollar was then devalued against gold. See gold. Mexico, 18 March 1938. Mexico expropriated foreign oil companies. Compensation was settled later: about $29 million to American firms in 1942, and $130 million to British interests in 1947. Russia's reserves, 2022. After the invasion of Ukraine, Western countries froze about $300 billion of the Russian central bank's reserves, roughly half of its foreign currency and gold. Even a central bank's reserves held abroad are subject to the host countries' law. What it kills Assets the state can see and reach: real estate, bank deposits, shares in strategic companies, whole businesses in an industry the state nationalises, and gold where its ownership is recorded or known. human capital and social capital are the hardest to confiscate, which is why the Cartography treats them as the root store. Key rule: every asset is held under someone's law. Know whose, and whether that law protects owners like you. Check yourself Q: Why can holding assets abroad both reduce and add confiscation risk? A: It moves them out of reach of your own government, but puts them under the law of the country that holds them, as Russia's frozen reserves showed in 2022. Q: How is confiscation different from theft? A: It's done openly by a state under its own laws, so courts may be unable to reverse it, and any compensation is set by the same state.",
