   "html": "<h2>What it is</h2>\n<p><strong>Custodial crypto</strong> is crypto held for you by a firm: an exchange account, a broker, or a fund that holds coins. The firm controls the keys. What you own is the firm's promise to give you the coins.</p>\n<h2>Why people use it</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Convenience:</strong> buy, sell and move between currency and crypto in one place.</li>\n<li><strong>No key management:</strong> no seed phrase to lose.</li>\n<li><strong>Familiar wrappers:</strong> some markets offer funds that hold crypto and trade like shares, through an ordinary brokerage account.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>How it works</h2>\n<p>In the <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/claims-and-liabilities\" data-page=\"claims-and-liabilities\">claims</a> sense, a custodial balance is the firm's liability to you. What protects it depends on the firm and where it operates:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>whether customer coins are kept <strong>separate</strong> from the firm's own;</li>\n<li>whether the firm publishes <strong>proof of reserves</strong> (evidence it holds what it owes);</li>\n<li>how a <strong>bankruptcy</strong> would treat customers.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What goes wrong</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Exchange collapse.</strong> In November 2022 the exchange FTX collapsed after customer funds were found to have been misused, and customers became creditors in a long bankruptcy. Mt. Gox, once the largest Bitcoin exchange, failed in 2014 after losing customers' coins.</li>\n<li><strong>Freezes:</strong> withdrawals can be halted in a crisis.</li>\n<li><strong>Hacks</strong> of the exchange's own wallets.</li>\n<li><strong>Rehypothecation:</strong> some platforms lend out customer coins to earn income, which adds a second layer of risk.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Key rule:</strong> \"not your keys, not your coins\". A custodial balance is a claim, so judge the firm the way you would judge a bank.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2>Connects to</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/bank-deposits\" data-page=\"bank-deposits\">Bank Deposits</a> — the traditional version of the same kind of claim, usually with more protection.</li>\n<li><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/brokers\" data-page=\"brokers\">Brokers</a> and <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/custody\" data-page=\"custody\">Custody</a> — how custody works in traditional markets.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>How it has failed</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mt. Gox, 2014.</strong> Once the largest Bitcoin exchange, it collapsed after losing customers' coins, and repayments took about a decade.</li>\n<li><strong>Celsius, 2022.</strong> The crypto lender froze withdrawals in June 2022 and filed for bankruptcy the next month. Customers who had been promised high yields became creditors.</li>\n<li><strong>FTX, 2022.</strong> Customer claims were valued at prices on the bankruptcy date, 11 November 2022, near a market low. By the time repayments began in 2025, a bitcoin claim was worth about a fifth of what one bitcoin then cost. Customers got their money back in dollars, but missed the recovery.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Check yourself</h2>\n<div class=\"qa\"><p class=\"q\">Why is a custodial balance closer to a bank deposit than to coins in your own wallet?</p><details><summary>Show answer</summary><p>Because in both cases you hold a claim on a firm that holds the asset. Your money is only as safe as the firm and the rules protecting customers.</p></details></div>",
