   "html": "<h2>What it is</h2>\n<p>An <strong>exchange</strong> is a marketplace where buyers and sellers of shares, bonds, futures, options or crypto meet under a common set of rules. Well-known examples include the New York Stock Exchange, Nasdaq, the London Stock Exchange and futures exchanges such as CME.</p>\n<h2>Why it exists</h2>\n<p>Bringing all buyers and sellers to one place makes it easier to find someone to trade with and to find a fair price. Exchanges also set listing rules for companies, publish prices, and supervise trading.</p>\n<h2>How it works</h2>\n<p><strong>The order book.</strong> Buy orders (<strong>bids</strong>) and sell orders (<strong>asks</strong>) are listed by price. A trade happens when a bid meets an ask.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The gap between the highest bid and the lowest ask is the <strong>spread</strong>. A tight spread means a liquid market.</li>\n<li>A <strong>market order</strong> buys or sells immediately at the best available price.</li>\n<li>A <strong>limit order</strong> sets the worst price you'll accept, and waits until it can be filled.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Market makers</strong> post bids and asks all day, earning the spread in return for being ready to trade. They're why you can usually sell a large share in seconds. See <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/market-making\" data-page=\"market-making\">Dealers and Market Making</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Fictional order book:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Bids (buyers)</th>\n<th>Price</th>\n<th>Asks (sellers)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td>100.20</td>\n<td>500 shares</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td>100.10</td>\n<td>300 shares</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>400 shares</td>\n<td>100.00</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>600 shares</td>\n<td>99.90</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The spread is 0.10. A market order to buy 500 shares fills 300 at 100.10 and 200 at 100.20, so its average price is 100.14: bigger orders \"walk the book\".</p>\n<h2>What goes wrong</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Liquidity vanishes in stress.</strong> Market makers step back, spreads widen and prices gap.</li>\n<li><strong>Flash crashes.</strong> On 6 May 2010 US share prices plunged and rebounded within minutes. Exchanges now use <strong>circuit breakers</strong> that pause trading after very large moves.</li>\n<li><strong>Market orders in thin markets</strong> can fill far from the last price.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Key rule:</strong> in thin or fast markets, use limit orders, so you decide the worst price you'll accept.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2>Connects to</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/brokers\" data-page=\"brokers\">Brokers</a> — who sends your orders to the exchange.</li>\n<li><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/settlement\" data-page=\"settlement\">Settlement</a> — what happens after the trade.</li>\n<li><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/liquidity\" data-page=\"liquidity\">Liquidity</a> — what an exchange provides, and what can disappear.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Check yourself</h2>\n<div class=\"qa\"><p class=\"q\">The best bid is 50.00 and the best ask is 50.05. What is the spread, and what does a small market buy order pay?</p><details><summary>Show answer</summary><p>The spread is 0.05, and a small market buy order pays 50.05, the lowest ask.</p></details></div>",
