   "text": "What it is An exchange is a marketplace where buyers and sellers of shares, bonds, futures, options or crypto meet under a common set of rules. Well known examples include the New York Stock Exchange, Nasdaq, the London Stock Exchange and futures exchanges such as CME. Why it exists Bringing all buyers and sellers to one place makes it easier to find someone to trade with and to find a fair price. Exchanges also set listing rules for companies, publish prices, and supervise trading. How it works The order book. Buy orders ( bids ) and sell orders ( asks ) are listed by price. A trade happens when a bid meets an ask. The gap between the highest bid and the lowest ask is the spread . A tight spread means a liquid market. A market order buys or sells immediately at the best available price. A limit order sets the worst price you'll accept, and waits until it can be filled. Market makers post bids and asks all day, earning the spread in return for being ready to trade. They're why you can usually sell a large share in seconds. See market making. Fictional order book: Bids (buyers) Price Asks (sellers) 100.20 500 shares 100.10 300 shares 400 shares 100.00 600 shares 99.90 The spread is 0.10. A market order to buy 500 shares fills 300 at 100.10 and 200 at 100.20, so its average price is 100.14: bigger orders \"walk the book\". What goes wrong Liquidity vanishes in stress. Market makers step back, spreads widen and prices gap. Flash crashes. On 6 May 2010 US share prices plunged and rebounded within minutes. Exchanges now use circuit breakers that pause trading after very large moves. Market orders in thin markets can fill far from the last price. Key rule: in thin or fast markets, use limit orders, so you decide the worst price you'll accept. Connects to brokers — who sends your orders to the exchange. settlement — what happens after the trade. liquidity — what an exchange provides, and what can disappear. Check yourself Q: The best bid is 50.00 and the best ask is 50.05. What is the spread, and what does a small market buy order pay? A: The spread is 0.05, and a small market buy order pays 50.05, the lowest ask.",
