   "adv": false,
   "html": "<h2>What it is</h2>\n<p><strong>Human capital</strong> is the value of what you can do: your skills, knowledge, health, energy and judgment. Economists measure it as the present value of the income those abilities are likely to earn over your working life. The term was made popular by the economist Gary Becker in the 1960s.</p>\n<h2>Why it matters</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>It's usually most of your wealth when you're young.</strong> A new graduate may have little money but decades of earnings ahead.</li>\n<li><strong>It turns into the other stores.</strong> Over a career, wages saved become deposits, bonds, shares and property.</li>\n<li><strong>It can rebuild from zero.</strong> A trained engineer who loses every asset still has the skill that earned them.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>How it grows</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Learning:</strong> study, practice and on-the-job experience.</li>\n<li><strong>Health:</strong> sleep, fitness and avoiding injury. A skill you can't use because you are ill earns nothing.</li>\n<li><strong>Choosing what to learn:</strong> skills that are scarce and in demand earn more, and skills that transfer between employers or industries are safer.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What goes wrong</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Death or incapacity</strong>, the killer on the <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/cartography-of-wealth\" data-page=\"cartography-of-wealth\">Cartography of Wealth</a>. It can't be transferred, so it ends with you.</li>\n<li><strong>Obsolescence:</strong> technology or markets stop paying for a skill.</li>\n<li><strong>One-industry exposure:</strong> a career built on a single employer or sector carries that sector's risk.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Key rule:</strong> human capital is protected by insurance, by health and by learning skills that transfer.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2>Connects to</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/social-capital\" data-page=\"social-capital\">Social Capital</a> — the other half of tier 1.</li>\n<li><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/time-value-of-money\" data-page=\"time-value-of-money\">Time Value of Money</a> — future earnings are worth less today the further away they are.</li>\n<li><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/diversification\" data-page=\"diversification\">Diversification</a> — invest away from the risks your job already carries.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>How it has failed</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Disability.</strong> The US Social Security Administration estimates that more than one in four 20-year-olds will become disabled before reaching retirement age.</li>\n<li><strong>Skills made obsolete.</strong> Whole occupations, such as typesetters and switchboard operators, have largely disappeared as technology changed; workers had to retrain or lose income.</li>\n<li><strong>One-industry towns.</strong> Britain's coal industry employed nearly 1.1 million people in 1913; its last deep mine, Kellingley, closed in December 2015. When a region's main industry closes, skills built for it can lose much of their value at once.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Check yourself</h2>\n<div class=\"qa\"><p class=\"q\">Why do people buy disability insurance?</p><details><summary>Show answer</summary><p>Because it converts some of their human capital, which they can't otherwise protect or sell, into money if they can no longer work.</p></details></div>",
