   "stub": false,
   "text": "What it is The common picture is that banks take in deposits and lend them out. In practice it mostly works the other way round: when a bank makes a loan, it creates a new deposit in the borrower's account. Money is created by the stroke of a pen. The Bank of England set this out plainly in a widely cited bulletin, Money creation in the modern economy . How it works 1. A bank approves a loan of 100. 2. It records a new asset (the loan) and a new liability (a deposit of 100 in the borrower's account). 3. The borrower spends the deposit, which moves to other banks, but it still exists as a deposit somewhere. 4. When the loan is repaid, the deposit is destroyed. What limits it Demand for loans from creditworthy borrowers. Profitability: the bank must earn more on the loan than it pays to fund it. Regulation: capital and liquidity rules. Central bank policy rates , which set the cost of funding. Key rule: lending creates deposits, and repaying loans destroys them. Credit booms expand the money supply; credit busts shrink it.",
