   "adv": false,
   "html": "<h2>What it is</h2>\n<p>A <strong>private business</strong> is a company whose shares aren't listed on a stock exchange. You might own one by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>running it yourself:</strong> a shop, a trade, a consultancy;</li>\n<li><strong>owning a family business</strong>, or a stake in a friend's;</li>\n<li><strong>investing early</strong> in a start-up as an angel investor;</li>\n<li><strong>investing through a fund:</strong> private equity buys established companies, venture capital backs young ones.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Why people own it</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The highest upside.</strong> A successful business can grow far beyond what you put in.</li>\n<li><strong>Control.</strong> You decide how it's run, at least if you own most of it.</li>\n<li><strong>Turning personal capital into an asset.</strong> A business is often the owner's skills and relationships packaged into something that can be sold. See <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/personal-capital\" data-page=\"personal-capital\">Human &amp; Social Capital</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>How it works</h2>\n<p><strong>Valuing it.</strong> There is no daily price. Buyers usually pay a <strong>multiple of profits</strong>, then knock off a discount because the stake is hard to sell, and often another if it depends heavily on one person. Every sale is a negotiation.</p>\n<p><strong>Private equity funds</strong> typically lock investors' money up for around ten years, buy companies, often with a lot of borrowed money, try to improve them, and sell. Fees are high, commonly a yearly management fee plus a share of the profits.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Listed shares</th>\n<th>Private business</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Price</strong></td>\n<td>Every trading day</td>\n<td>At sale or appraisal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Selling</strong></td>\n<td>Seconds</td>\n<td>Months, if a buyer exists</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Information</strong></td>\n<td>Public, audited</td>\n<td>What the owners choose to share</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Spread</strong></td>\n<td>Easy across hundreds</td>\n<td>Often one company</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2>What goes wrong</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Concentration and management</strong>, the killer on the <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/cartography-of-wealth\" data-page=\"cartography-of-wealth\">Cartography of Wealth</a>. One bad decision, one lost customer, or one key person leaving can end it.</li>\n<li><strong>Most new businesses don't last.</strong> Of US business locations opened in March 2013, 79.6% were still operating a year later, 50.6% after five years and 34.7% after ten.</li>\n<li><strong>No exit.</strong> Owners often find there is no buyer at the price they hoped for, or that the business is worth little without them.</li>\n<li><strong>Everything in one place.</strong> An owner's income, savings and often their home (as security for a loan) can all depend on the same business.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Key rule:</strong> a private business can be your best asset and your least diversified one. Build savings outside it, and make it able to run without you.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2>Connects to</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/equities\" data-page=\"equities\">Equities</a> — the branch this sits in.</li>\n<li><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/valuation\" data-page=\"valuation\">Valuation</a> — how buyers put a price on it.</li>\n<li><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/human-capital\" data-page=\"human-capital\">Human Capital</a> and <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/social-capital\" data-page=\"social-capital\">Social Capital</a> — what most small businesses are made of.</li>\n<li><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/leverage\" data-page=\"leverage\">Leverage</a> — how private equity boosts its returns, and its risks.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>How it has failed</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Most don't last.</strong> Of US business locations opened in March 2013, only 34.7% were still operating ten years later.</li>\n<li><strong>A big customer fails.</strong> When the UK construction company Carillion went into liquidation in January 2018, around 30,000 suppliers were owed about £1 billion. Small firms that depended on it lost their main customer and their unpaid invoices together.</li>\n<li><strong>The owner leaves.</strong> Many small businesses can't be sold because much of their value walks out of the door with the owner.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Check yourself</h2>\n<div class=\"qa\"><p class=\"q\">Why do buyers often pay less for a business that depends on its owner?</p><details><summary>Show answer</summary><p>Because much of its value may walk out of the door when the owner leaves. The buyer is paying for profits that might not continue without them.</p></details></div>",
