   "adv": false,
   "html": "<h2>What it is</h2>\n<p><strong>Real estate</strong> is land and the buildings on it: the home you live in, flats you rent out, offices, shops and warehouses. Ownership is recorded in a public <strong>title registry</strong>, which is what makes it the most legible store of wealth on the <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/cartography-of-wealth\" data-page=\"cartography-of-wealth\">Cartography of Wealth</a>.</p>\n<h2>Why people own it</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Use.</strong> A home is both an asset and a place to live; owning it replaces rent.</li>\n<li><strong>Income.</strong> Rented property can pay a regular income.</li>\n<li><strong>Leverage.</strong> Property is the one asset most people can buy mostly with borrowed money, through a <strong>mortgage</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Inflation.</strong> Rents and building costs tend to rise with prices over long periods.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>How it works</h2>\n<p><strong>Leverage cuts both ways.</strong> A buyer puts down a deposit and borrows the rest. Gains and losses are on the whole price, but they land on the deposit.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fictional example</th>\n<th>Price</th>\n<th>Loan</th>\n<th>Your equity</th>\n<th>Change on your deposit</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>You buy</td>\n<td>400,000</td>\n<td>320,000</td>\n<td>80,000</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Price rises 10%</td>\n<td>440,000</td>\n<td>320,000</td>\n<td>120,000</td>\n<td><strong>+50%</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Price falls 10%</td>\n<td>360,000</td>\n<td>320,000</td>\n<td>40,000</td>\n<td><strong>−50%</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Price falls 20%</td>\n<td>320,000</td>\n<td>320,000</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td><strong>−100%</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>A 20% deposit turns a 10% price move into a 50% move on your money. That is before buying costs, interest, repairs and tax, which all reduce the gain or deepen the loss. See <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/leverage\" data-page=\"leverage\">Leverage</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Costs of owning:</strong> purchase taxes and fees, mortgage interest, maintenance, insurance, property taxes and, for landlords, empty months between tenants.</p>\n<h2>What goes wrong</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Immobility</strong>, the killer on the Cartography of Wealth. The one asset that can't move can be taxed, rent-controlled, rezoned or compulsorily purchased where it stands.</li>\n<li><strong>Price falls with a mortgage.</strong> In the 2008 crisis, US house prices fell by roughly a quarter to a third nationally, depending on the index, and many owners ended up owing more than their homes were worth.</li>\n<li><strong>Slow sales.</strong> Selling takes weeks to months and costs a few percent of the price.</li>\n<li><strong>Concentration.</strong> One property is one location, one local economy and often most of a household's wealth.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Key rule:</strong> property's risk is mostly in the loan. Know what a 20% price fall would do to your deposit before you buy.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2>Connects to</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/reits\" data-page=\"reits\">REITs</a> — owning property through shares instead of a title.</li>\n<li><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/real-assets\" data-page=\"real-assets\">Real Assets</a> — the branch this sits in.</li>\n<li><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/credit-risk\" data-page=\"credit-risk\">Credit Risk and Ratings</a> — mortgages are the largest loans most banks make.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>How it has failed</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Japan after 1990.</strong> When Japan's property bubble burst in the early 1990s, commercial land prices in its big cities kept falling until the mid-2000s.</li>\n<li><strong>The US, 2006–12.</strong> House prices fell by roughly a quarter to a third nationally, and millions of homes were lost to foreclosure.</li>\n<li><strong>The state's claim never stops.</strong> Property taxes are due every year whatever the market does, and land can be compulsorily purchased, rezoned or seized, because it can't be moved.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Check yourself</h2>\n<div class=\"qa\"><p class=\"q\">With a 25% deposit, roughly what happens to your equity if the property's price falls 25%?</p><details><summary>Show answer</summary><p>It is wiped out. The price has fallen by the whole deposit, so you owe as much as the home is worth, before any costs.</p></details></div>",
