   "text": "What it is Real estate is land and the buildings on it: the home you live in, flats you rent out, offices, shops and warehouses. Ownership is recorded in a public title registry , which is what makes it the most legible store of wealth on the Cartography of Wealth. Why people own it Use. A home is both an asset and a place to live; owning it replaces rent. Income. Rented property can pay a regular income. Leverage. Property is the one asset most people can buy mostly with borrowed money, through a mortgage . Inflation. Rents and building costs tend to rise with prices over long periods. How it works Leverage cuts both ways. A buyer puts down a deposit and borrows the rest. Gains and losses are on the whole price, but they land on the deposit. Fictional example Price Loan Your equity Change on your deposit You buy 400,000 320,000 80,000 — Price rises 10% 440,000 320,000 120,000 +50% Price falls 10% 360,000 320,000 40,000 −50% Price falls 20% 320,000 320,000 0 −100% A 20% deposit turns a 10% price move into a 50% move on your money. That is before buying costs, interest, repairs and tax, which all reduce the gain or deepen the loss. See leverage. Costs of owning: purchase taxes and fees, mortgage interest, maintenance, insurance, property taxes and, for landlords, empty months between tenants. What goes wrong Immobility , the killer on the Cartography of Wealth. The one asset that can't move can be taxed, rent controlled, rezoned or compulsorily purchased where it stands. Price falls with a mortgage. In the 2008 crisis, US house prices fell by roughly a quarter to a third nationally, depending on the index, and many owners ended up owing more than their homes were worth. Slow sales. Selling takes weeks to months and costs a few percent of the price. Concentration. One property is one location, one local economy and often most of a household's wealth. Key rule: property's risk is mostly in the loan. Know what a 20% price fall would do to your deposit before you buy. Connects to reits — owning property through shares instead of a title. real assets — the branch this sits in. credit risk — mortgages are the largest loans most banks make. How it has failed Japan after 1990. When Japan's property bubble burst in the early 1990s, commercial land prices in its big cities kept falling until the mid 2000s. The US, 2006–12. House prices fell by roughly a quarter to a third nationally, and millions of homes were lost to foreclosure. The state's claim never stops. Property taxes are due every year whatever the market does, and land can be compulsorily purchased, rezoned or seized, because it can't be moved. Check yourself Q: With a 25% deposit, roughly what happens to your equity if the property's price falls 25%? A: It is wiped out. The price has fallen by the whole deposit, so you owe as much as the home is worth, before any costs.",
