   "html": "<h2>What it is</h2>\n<p><strong>Self-custody</strong> means holding the keys to your own coins in a wallet only you control. See <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/keys-and-wallets\" data-page=\"keys-and-wallets\">Keys and Wallets</a> for how keys work.</p>\n<h2>Why people choose it</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>No custodian risk:</strong> an exchange failing can't take your coins.</li>\n<li><strong>No freezes:</strong> nobody can block your coins without your keys.</li>\n<li><strong>Privacy:</strong> the coins aren't held in an account under your name.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is the digital version of keeping gold in your own safe: you remove the middleman, and become the security team.</p>\n<h2>How to do it well</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hardware wallet</strong> for any meaningful amount.</li>\n<li><strong>Seed phrase backed up offline</strong>, in more than one secure place, never photographed or typed into a website.</li>\n<li><strong>Test a recovery</strong> with a small amount before you rely on it.</li>\n<li><strong>Multisig</strong> for large amounts, so one lost or stolen key isn't fatal.</li>\n<li><strong>An inheritance plan</strong> that lets someone you trust recover the coins, without making them easy to steal.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What goes wrong</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lost seed phrase:</strong> coins locked forever.</li>\n<li><strong>Leaked seed phrase:</strong> coins stolen, with no recourse.</li>\n<li><strong>Phishing and malicious approvals.</strong> See <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/keys-and-wallets\" data-page=\"keys-and-wallets\">Keys and Wallets</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Protocol failure:</strong> self-custody protects you from firms failing, not from the chain or token failing.</li>\n<li><strong>Physical threats:</strong> people known to hold crypto can be targeted directly.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Key rule:</strong> self-custody swaps counterparty risk for your own operational risk. Only do it with a backup you have tested.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2>How it has failed</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lost keys.</strong> In 2020 the analytics firm Chainalysis estimated that about 3.7 million bitcoin, roughly a fifth of the supply then mined, hadn't moved in at least five years and was probably lost for good.</li>\n<li><strong>Deep falls.</strong> Bitcoin has fallen by more than three-quarters from its peak more than once, including in 2017–18 and 2021–22. Holding your own keys protects you from a custodian, not from the price.</li>\n<li><strong>No one to call.</strong> Coins sent to a wrong address, or signed away to a scam, can't be reversed. See <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/keys-and-wallets\" data-page=\"keys-and-wallets\">Keys and Wallets</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Check yourself</h2>\n<div class=\"qa\"><p class=\"q\">What does self-custody protect you from, and what doesn't it?</p><details><summary>Show answer</summary><p>It protects you from a custodian failing, freezing or being hacked. It doesn't protect you from losing your keys, being tricked into signing, or the coin itself failing.</p></details></div>",
