   "html": "<h2>What it is</h2>\n<p>A <strong>share</strong> (or <strong>stock</strong>) is one unit of ownership in a company. Owning shares in a listed company gives you:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a claim on part of its profits, paid as <strong>dividends</strong> if the board decides to pay them;</li>\n<li>a <strong>vote</strong> on major decisions such as electing the board;</li>\n<li>the <strong>residual claim</strong>: what's left if the company is sold or wound up, after all creditors are paid.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Why it exists</h2>\n<p>Selling shares lets a company raise money it never has to repay. A listing on a stock exchange lets owners sell to someone else at any time, which makes people far more willing to invest in the first place.</p>\n<h2>How it works</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Going public.</strong> A company first sells shares to the public in an <strong>initial public offering</strong> (IPO). After that, shares change hands between investors on <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/exchanges\" data-page=\"exchanges\">Exchanges</a>; the company isn't involved.</li>\n<li><strong>Returns.</strong> You earn from dividends and from changes in the share price. Some companies pay no dividend and reinvest everything; others buy back their own shares.</li>\n<li><strong>Holding.</strong> Shares bought through a broker are usually held electronically, often in the broker's name for your benefit (a <strong>nominee</strong> account). See <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/brokers\" data-page=\"brokers\">Brokers</a> and <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/custody\" data-page=\"custody\">Custody</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"fig\"><a class=\"fig-link\" href=\"assets/figures/shares-ownership-chain.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"assets/figures/shares-ownership-chain.svg\" alt=\"A chain from you, to your broker, to the central depository, to the company. Your legal claim is on your broker, not directly on the company.\" width=\"560\" height=\"700\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><figcaption>Who actually holds a share bought through a broker. Simplified; details vary by country. See Custody for the full chain.<span class=\"fig-hint\"> Tap to enlarge.</span></figcaption></figure>\n<h2>What goes wrong</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The company fails.</strong> Shares can go to zero, because shareholders are paid last. See <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/claims-and-liabilities\" data-page=\"claims-and-liabilities\">Claims and Liabilities</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>The market falls.</strong> Even strong companies' shares drop in a crash. The S&amp;P 500 lost about 57% from October 2007 to March 2009.</li>\n<li><strong>The broker fails.</strong> Your shares should be held separately from the broker's own money, and many countries have a protection scheme. In the US, SIPC covers up to $500,000 per customer (including up to $250,000 of cash) if a broker fails and customer assets are missing. It does not cover a fall in share prices. This is the counterparty killer on the <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/cartography-of-wealth\" data-page=\"cartography-of-wealth\">Cartography of Wealth</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Dilution.</strong> If a company issues new shares, each existing share owns a smaller slice.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Key rule:</strong> a share is a residual claim held through an intermediary. Diversify across companies, and know how your broker holds your shares.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2>Connects to</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/equities\" data-page=\"equities\">Equities</a> — the branch this sits in.</li>\n<li><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/indices\" data-page=\"indices\">Indices</a> and <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/funds-and-etfs\" data-page=\"funds-and-etfs\">Funds and ETFs</a> — ways to own many shares at once.</li>\n<li><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/valuation\" data-page=\"valuation\">Valuation</a> — what a share might be worth.</li>\n<li><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/settlement\" data-page=\"settlement\">Settlement</a> — how a trade completes after you click buy.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>How it has failed</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Wiped out, 1917 and 1949.</strong> After the Russian revolution and the Communist victory in China, stock markets closed and shareholders lost everything. Long-run studies of share returns mostly measure markets that survived.</li>\n<li><strong>The 1929 crash.</strong> The Dow Jones Industrial Average lost about 89% between 1929 and 1932, and didn't regain its 1929 peak until 1954.</li>\n<li><strong>Japan, 1989–2024.</strong> The Nikkei 225 closed at 38,915.87 on 29 December 1989. It didn't close higher until 22 February 2024, 34 years later.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Check yourself</h2>\n<div class=\"qa\"><p class=\"q\">Your broker goes bust, and separately your shares have fallen 30% in a market slump. Which of these would SIPC help with in the US?</p><details><summary>Show answer</summary><p>Only the broker failure, and only if customer assets are missing, up to the limits. SIPC doesn't protect against prices falling.</p></details></div>",
