   "adv": false,
   "html": "<h2>What it is</h2>\n<p>A <strong>stablecoin</strong> is a token designed to keep a steady value, usually one US dollar. It brings dollars onto blockchains, where they can move at any hour and be used by programs.</p>\n<h2>Why it matters</h2>\n<p>Stablecoins are the main bridge between crypto and ordinary money. In the <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/hierarchy-of-money\" data-page=\"hierarchy-of-money\">hierarchy of money</a> they sit high up: a private promise to pay dollars, like a bank deposit but without the same protections in most places.</p>\n<h2>How it works</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Design</th>\n<th>What keeps it at a dollar</th>\n<th>Whose liability?</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Reserve-backed</strong></td>\n<td>The issuer holds cash and short-term government debt and redeems tokens for dollars</td>\n<td>The issuing company's</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Crypto-collateralised</strong></td>\n<td>Loans backed by more crypto than they're worth, closed automatically if the collateral falls</td>\n<td>A program's, backed by borrowers' collateral</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Algorithmic</strong></td>\n<td>A mechanism that mints and burns a partner token to hold the price</td>\n<td>Nobody's — it relies on confidence</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Algorithmic designs have failed badly.</strong> In May 2022 the TerraUSD stablecoin lost its dollar peg and collapsed within days, taking its partner token with it.</p>\n<h2>What goes wrong</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>De-peg:</strong> if holders doubt the issuer can pay, they sell, and the price drops below one dollar. That is a run, the same as on a bank.</li>\n<li><strong>Reserve risk:</strong> the backing may be weaker, less liquid or less transparent than claimed.</li>\n<li><strong>Freezes:</strong> many reserve-backed issuers can freeze tokens at specific addresses.</li>\n<li><strong>Who it hits:</strong> everyone holding the token, whether on an exchange (<a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/custodial-crypto\" data-page=\"custodial-crypto\">Custodial Crypto</a>) or in their own wallet (<a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/self-custody\" data-page=\"self-custody\">Self-Custody</a>). Holding your own keys protects you from the exchange, not from the issuer's promise.</li>\n<li><strong>Copies on other chains:</strong> a stablecoin copied by a fork, or bridged by a third party, is not the issuer's promise. See <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/forks\" data-page=\"forks\">Forks</a> and <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/wrapped-tokens\" data-page=\"wrapped-tokens\">Wrapped and Bridged Tokens</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Key rule:</strong> a stablecoin is only as good as what it can be redeemed for, and who promises to redeem it.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2>Connects to</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/bank-deposits\" data-page=\"bank-deposits\">Bank Deposits</a> — the closest traditional comparison.</li>\n<li><a class=\"wl\" href=\"#/map/p/t-bills\" data-page=\"t-bills\">Treasury Bills</a> — what many reserve-backed issuers hold.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Check yourself</h2>\n<div class=\"qa\"><p class=\"q\">In hierarchy-of-money terms, what is happening when a stablecoin trades at 0.95 dollars?</p><details><summary>Show answer</summary><p>Holders doubt the promise, so they sell the private token (upper layer) to get dollars (lower layer). The 5% gap is the market's discount on the issuer's promise.</p></details></div>",
